ually tracked the numbers and  found out that they ended up having to  hire more  of the much higher paid executives to  get the same amount of work done because  so much of their day now was spent doing  things like word processors and  communication etc  that the salary cost went up so they  save salary costs by firing support  staff  but then increase salary costs by having  to hire more people to get the same  amount of work done and they ended up  worse off because these salaries were  higher  i think we missed the mark on the  digital productivity revolution as  technology made certain  administrative tasks easier the right  move was not to  move all of that work onto the plate of  the the executives or frontline value  producers it should have been to make it  cheaper to have support staff i think  the right application of that of that  technology would be oh fewer support  staff can now support  more of the frontline workers that was  probably the right way to  to approach the productivity revolution  in terms of maximizing return instead we  said  let's fire the support workers and make  everyone a part-time administrative  assistant and a part-time whatever their  main job is  we know the way the brain works that  that is a that was a bad mix so i think  we really missed the mark on this  digital productivity  revolution it does make us much more  productive  if we use that to actually empower  support staff to be even more effective  not to put more things on the plate of  the people that you wanted thinking  about the ad copy or making the  executive decisions or writing the  academic papers or whatever it is you  had hired them to do  right uh that it's it's a fascinating  book  the book is a world without email  reimagining work in an age of  communication overload you know i find  that so often  when i talk to people about this book  other books you've written and and other  there's this visceral reaction like that  would never work we shouldn't even be  thinking about that do you do you see  is that is that a common reaction from a  lot of people well i think the key thing  with this book is  understanding that you're changing the  workflow because as soon as you say just  you need to spend less time on email  they're so overwhelmed by this  hyperactive hive mind workflow like are  you kidding me  i i would be dead because everything is  happening in the inbox but the real aha  moment for most people is when you say  yeah the reason why that's happening is  because the underlying agreements by how  work happens is generating all these  messages  and it doesn't have to be that way and  you tell them about companies where they  don't use email  and they don't use slack and they don't  have an inbox to check and they do one  thing at a time and maybe they work on  three things each day and when you show  them those stories  you can just see the light turn on  behind their eyes so  i think that's key the way we work today  makes it impossible not to check email i  agree  we can fix the underlying way we work  though we've been speaking with cal  newport computer science professor at  georgetown university will link to the  latest book a world without email  cal always great having you on thanks  david
 

